{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558827097,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40a9673001GJY9xZH2T0R710","sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","messageID":"msg_1d40a7bde001MmedH8TJGUvCuM","type":"tool","id":"call_be725e08254649b6b7fa50ca","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558826099,"end":1794558827097}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558827101,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40a9500001u1CHSqpr4qu1me","sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","messageID":"msg_1d40a7bde001MmedH8TJGUvCuM","type":"tool","id":"call_84679416424646f3a4874694","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md, lines 1-176\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code\n3: description: \"Implement, debug and verify code: TypeScript, Python, shell, JSON; quality and review workflow modes with stack surface packets.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 3.11.6.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n11: \n12: # Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n13: \n14: One skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\n15: The **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\n16: The **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\n17: Each packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\n18: This hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n19: \n20: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n21: `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n22: `ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\n23: The authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\n24: A packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n29: \n30: Use this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n31: \n32: **Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n33: \n34: | Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n35: |------|------------|--------|\n36: | **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n37: | **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n38: \n39: **Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n40: \n41: | Surface | Carries | Packet |\n42: |---------|---------|--------|\n43: | **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n44: | **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n45: | **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n46: \n47: **Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n48: - SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n49: - CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n50: - Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n51: \n52: The hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\n53: Any other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n54: \n55: ### When NOT to Use\n56: - Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n57: - Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n58: - Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n59: - For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n60: - When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n61: - Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n66: \n67: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n68: Stage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\n69: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\n70: Both files point back here instead of restating the split.\n71: \n72: The advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n73: Compiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n74: \n75: A bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\n76: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n77: \n78: ---\n79: \n80: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n81: \n82: ### Terms\n83: \n84: - **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n85: - **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n86: - **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n87: - **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n88: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n89: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n90: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n91: - **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n92: - **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n93: - **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n94: - **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n95: - **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n96: \n97: ### Layout\n98: ```\n99: sk-code/\n100:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n101:   README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n102:   ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n103:   mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n104:   hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n105:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n106:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n107:   leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n108:   changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n109:   feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n110:   manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n111:   benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n112:   sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n113:   sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n114:   sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n115:   sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n116:   sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n117:   shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n118: ```\n119: \n120: Each mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n121: \n122: ### Backend\n123: The `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\n124: It centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\n125: It carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\n126: The `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\n127: The `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\n128: The packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\n129: Shared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\n130: It must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n131: \n132: The **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\n133: Their surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\n134: Those files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\n135: A request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\n136: The acting agent applies that doctrine.\n137: \n138: ### Maintenance\n139: \n140: After adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n141: \n142: ---\n143: \n144: ## 4. RULES\n145: \n146: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n147: - **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n148: - **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n149: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n150: - **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n151: - **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n152: - **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n153: - **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n154: \n155: ### ⛔ NEVER\n156: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n157: - **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n158: - **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n159: - **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n160: \n161: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n162: - A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n163: - Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 5. REFERENCES\n168: \n169: - Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n170: - Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n171: - Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n172: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n173: - Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n174: - Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n175: - Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n176: - Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md, lines 1-176\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code\n3: description: \"Implement, debug and verify code: TypeScript, Python, shell, JSON; quality and review workflow modes with stack surface packets.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 3.11.6.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n11: \n12: # Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n13: \n14: One skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\n15: The **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\n16: The **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\n17: Each packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\n18: This hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n19: \n20: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n21: `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n22: `ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\n23: The authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\n24: A packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n29: \n30: Use this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n31: \n32: **Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n33: \n34: | Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n35: |------|------------|--------|\n36: | **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n37: | **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n38: \n39: **Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n40: \n41: | Surface | Carries | Packet |\n42: |---------|---------|--------|\n43: | **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n44: | **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n45: | **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n46: \n47: **Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n48: - SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n49: - CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n50: - Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n51: \n52: The hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\n53: Any other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n54: \n55: ### When NOT to Use\n56: - Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n57: - Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n58: - Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n59: - For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n60: - When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n61: - Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n66: \n67: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n68: Stage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\n69: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\n70: Both files point back here instead of restating the split.\n71: \n72: The advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n73: Compiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n74: \n75: A bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\n76: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n77: \n78: ---\n79: \n80: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n81: \n82: ### Terms\n83: \n84: - **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n85: - **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n86: - **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n87: - **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n88: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n89: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n90: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n91: - **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n92: - **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n93: - **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n94: - **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n95: - **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n96: \n97: ### Layout\n98: ```\n99: sk-code/\n100:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n101:   README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n102:   ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n103:   mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n104:   hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n105:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n106:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n107:   leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n108:   changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n109:   feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n110:   manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n111:   benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n112:   sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n113:   sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n114:   sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n115:   sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n116:   sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n117:   shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n118: ```\n119: \n120: Each mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n121: \n122: ### Backend\n123: The `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\n124: It centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\n125: It carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\n126: The `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\n127: The `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\n128: The packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\n129: Shared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\n130: It must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n131: \n132: The **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\n133: Their surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\n134: Those files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\n135: A request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\n136: The acting agent applies that doctrine.\n137: \n138: ### Maintenance\n139: \n140: After adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n141: \n142: ---\n143: \n144: ## 4. RULES\n145: \n146: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n147: - **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n148: - **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n149: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n150: - **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n151: - **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n152: - **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n153: - **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n154: \n155: ### ⛔ NEVER\n156: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n157: - **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n158: - **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n159: - **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n160: \n161: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n162: - A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n163: - Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 5. REFERENCES\n168: \n169: - Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n170: - Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n171: - Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n172: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n173: - Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n174: - Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n175: - Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n176: - Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558825728,"end":1794558827101}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558827105,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40a98f50012kcsinaI5wKTiO","sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","messageID":"msg_1d40a7bde001MmedH8TJGUvCuM","type":"tool","id":"call_904b1169705a48bcaddd6901","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558826741,"end":1794558827105}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794558827111,"sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d40a97b1001ENypp1wcFKn5ND","sessionID":"ses_e2bf67522ffeXDCV6Z2AUOqOyV","messageID":"msg_1d40a7bde001MmedH8TJGUvCuM","type":"tool","id":"call_f16b92a848fe4804873feb8c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794558826417,"end":1794558827111}}}}
